/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: AC-306 ICS/SCADA & Operational Technology (OT) Security */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["AC-306"] = {
ov:"Operational technology controls physical processes where availability and safety outrank confidentiality, and a breach can cause physical harm. This advanced course covers ICS/SCADA security: OT's distinct priorities, the Purdue model, industrial protocols, safety systems, segmentation, and the IT/OT convergence. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"OT versus IT: Different Priorities",d:"Why OT security is not just IT security.",
obj:["Explain how OT priorities differ from IT","Explain the availability-and-safety-first inversion","Describe the constraints that shape OT security"],
read:["Operational technology (OT) — the industrial control systems that run physical processes (power grids, water treatment, manufacturing, pipelines) — has fundamentally different priorities from IT, and treating OT like IT is a common, dangerous mistake. In IT, the CIA triad typically prioritizes confidentiality (protecting data); in OT, the priorities invert: availability and safety come first, because OT controls physical processes where downtime can halt critical infrastructure and a malfunction can cause physical harm — injury, environmental damage, or death. An OT system that stops (availability loss) can shut down a power plant; an OT system that malfunctions (safety/integrity loss) can cause an explosion. Confidentiality, while it matters, is usually the lowest priority.",
"This inversion, plus OT's constraints, shapes a distinct security discipline. OT systems often run legacy technology (decades-old controllers that cannot be patched or hardened conventionally — the unpatchable-legacy problem from BC-103/IC-210), have strict availability requirements (they cannot be taken down for patching or that halts the physical process), operate on long lifecycles (equipment lasting decades), and control real-world physical processes with safety implications. Security measures that are routine in IT (patching, rebooting, aggressive scanning) can be dangerous in OT (disrupting the physical process, the safety-critical concern). So OT security adapts: prioritizing availability and safety, relying heavily on segmentation (since the systems cannot be hardened — the primary defense from IC-210), and being extremely careful about anything that could disrupt the physical process. Understanding OT's different priorities — availability and safety first, with legacy and physical-process constraints — frames why OT security is a distinct discipline, not just IT security applied to industrial systems, and why the approaches must differ."],
ex:"A power utility's OT engineers reject a proposal to routinely patch and scan the control systems as IT does — because patching requires downtime that halts power generation (availability), scanning could disrupt the sensitive controllers (safety), and the legacy systems cannot be conventionally hardened anyway. Instead, OT security relies on segmentation and careful, availability-and-safety-preserving measures. The inverted priorities (availability and safety first) and the physical-process constraints made OT security a distinct discipline — IT approaches would have risked the physical process.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-82 Rev.3 (OT Security)","OT priorities and constraints."],["ISA/IEC 62443","Industrial automation and control systems security."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT priorities differ from IT in that OT prioritizes:",o:["Confidentiality first","Availability and safety first — because it controls physical processes where downtime and malfunction cause real harm","The same as IT","Nothing"],a:1,x:"OT inverts the typical IT priority: availability and safety come first because malfunction or downtime can cause physical harm."},
{q:"Routine IT security measures (patching, scanning) can be dangerous in OT because they:",o:["Are too slow","Can disrupt the physical process — downtime halts critical infrastructure, and scanning can disturb sensitive controllers","Improve safety","Are required"],a:1,x:"OT's availability and safety constraints mean IT-routine actions can disrupt the physical process, requiring adapted approaches."},
{q:"OT relies heavily on segmentation because:",o:["It is cheap","The legacy systems often cannot be hardened or patched conventionally — segmentation contains them (the primary OT defense)","It is fast","IT uses it"],a:1,x:"Unpatchable OT systems rely on segmentation for containment — the primary defense when conventional hardening is impossible (IC-210)."}]},
{t:"The Purdue Model",d:"The reference architecture for OT.",
obj:["Explain the Purdue model and its levels","Describe the segmentation the model implies","Explain the IT/OT boundary"],
read:["The Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture (the Purdue model) is the foundational reference for OT network architecture, organizing industrial systems into levels: Level 0 (the physical process — sensors and actuators), Level 1 (basic control — PLCs and controllers directly controlling the process), Level 2 (area supervisory control — HMIs and SCADA), Level 3 (site operations — manufacturing operations systems), Level 3.5 (the industrial DMZ — the boundary between OT and IT), and Levels 4-5 (the enterprise IT — business systems, the internet). The model provides a common framework for understanding and securing OT architecture, with the lower levels being the safety-and-availability-critical control systems and the higher levels being IT.",
"The Purdue model's security value is the segmentation and boundary structure it implies. The levels should be segmented, with controlled communication between them, and critically, the boundary between OT (Levels 0-3) and IT (Levels 4-5) — the industrial DMZ at Level 3.5 — is the most important security boundary in OT (the corporate-to-OT segmentation, the highest-value from BC-101/IC-210). Traffic between OT and IT should be tightly controlled and minimized through this DMZ, so that a compromise of the IT network (the more-exposed, internet-connected side) cannot easily reach the safety-critical OT control systems. Many OT attacks involve compromising IT and pivoting to OT (the hybrid-pivot concern), which the Purdue model's OT/IT segmentation is designed to prevent. Understanding the Purdue model — the reference architecture organizing OT into levels with the critical OT/IT boundary — frames the architectural foundation of OT security, the segmentation structure that isolates the safety-critical control systems from the exposed IT network, and the common language for OT security architecture."],
ex:"A manufacturer secures its OT per the Purdue model: the control systems (Levels 0-2) are segmented from site operations (Level 3), and critically, the OT/IT boundary (Level 3.5 industrial DMZ) tightly controls and minimizes traffic between the OT control network and the corporate IT/internet. When the corporate IT network is later compromised, the OT/IT segmentation at the industrial DMZ prevents the attacker from reaching the safety-critical control systems. The Purdue model's segmentation structure — especially the OT/IT boundary — isolated the critical control systems from the exposed IT side.",
refs:[["Purdue model and NIST SP 800-82","OT reference architecture."],["ISA/IEC 62443 zones and conduits","OT segmentation."]],
kc:[
{q:"The Purdue model organizes OT into:",o:["A flat network","Levels — from the physical process (Level 0) up through control and supervision to the enterprise IT (Levels 4-5)","Two systems","No structure"],a:1,x:"The Purdue levels structure OT from the physical process up to enterprise IT, providing a framework for segmenting and securing the architecture."},
{q:"The most important security boundary in the Purdue model is:",o:["Within Level 0","The OT/IT boundary (the industrial DMZ at Level 3.5) — isolating the safety-critical control systems from the exposed IT network","There is none","Between Levels 4 and 5"],a:1,x:"The corporate-to-OT boundary is the highest-value segmentation; it prevents an IT compromise from reaching the safety-critical control systems."},
{q:"Many OT attacks involve:",o:["Only Level 0","Compromising the exposed IT network and pivoting to OT — which the Purdue OT/IT segmentation is designed to prevent","No pivoting","Only physical access"],a:1,x:"The IT-to-OT pivot is a common OT attack path; the Purdue model's OT/IT segmentation is designed to block it."}]},
{t:"ICS Components: PLCs, SCADA, DCS",d:"The systems that run the process.",
obj:["Identify the key ICS components and their functions","Explain PLCs, SCADA, HMIs, and DCS","Explain the security implications of these components"],
read:["Industrial control systems comprise several component types. Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs) are the workhorses at Level 1 — ruggedized computers that directly control physical equipment (opening valves, running motors) based on their programmed logic, reading sensors and driving actuators. SCADA (Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition) systems provide supervisory monitoring and control across a distributed process (a pipeline, a grid), collecting data from remote sites and allowing operators to monitor and control. Human-Machine Interfaces (HMIs) are the operator screens for monitoring and controlling the process. Distributed Control Systems (DCS) provide integrated control for a process (a plant), and RTUs (Remote Terminal Units) interface remote field devices. These components together run the physical process.",
"Their security implications flow from their nature. PLCs directly control physical equipment, so compromising a PLC means controlling the physical process (the Stuxnet attack manipulated PLCs to damage centrifuges — module five), yet PLCs often have weak or no security (designed for reliability in a trusted environment, not for defending against attackers — the legacy-and-trust problem). SCADA and HMIs, if compromised, let an attacker monitor and control the process. These components were largely designed before OT faced cyber threats, assuming a trusted, isolated environment, so they often lack the authentication, encryption, and hardening that IT systems have — making the segmentation and access control around them (isolating them from attackers) the primary defense, since the components themselves cannot be relied upon to defend against a determined attacker. Understanding the ICS components — PLCs, SCADA, HMIs, DCS and their functions and weak inherent security — frames what OT security is protecting: the systems that run the physical process, which directly control real-world equipment yet often lack inherent security, making their isolation and protection critical."],
ex:"An OT security assessment finds that the PLCs controlling a plant's process have no authentication (anyone who can reach them on the network can send them commands) and cannot be upgraded to add it — designed for a trusted, isolated environment. Because the PLCs directly control physical equipment and cannot defend themselves, the security relies on isolating them (segmentation, access control) so attackers cannot reach them. The components' weak inherent security made their isolation the primary defense — protecting the systems that run the physical process but cannot protect themselves.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-82 (ICS components)","PLCs, SCADA, DCS, and their security."],["ICS security fundamentals references","Component functions and weaknesses."]],
kc:[
{q:"Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs):",o:["Store business data","Directly control physical equipment based on programmed logic — compromising one means controlling the physical process","Are IT servers","Have no function"],a:1,x:"PLCs control real-world equipment; their compromise (as in Stuxnet) means controlling the physical process — yet they often lack security."},
{q:"ICS components often lack inherent security because they were:",o:["Recently designed","Designed before OT faced cyber threats, assuming a trusted, isolated environment — lacking authentication, encryption, and hardening","Over-secured","Never networked"],a:1,x:"Legacy ICS components assumed a trusted, isolated environment, so they lack the security IT systems have — making isolation the primary defense."},
{q:"Because ICS components cannot defend themselves, the primary defense is:",o:["Their own security","Isolating them — segmentation and access control so attackers cannot reach them","Nothing","Encryption on the PLC"],a:1,x:"Weak inherent security makes isolation (segmentation, access control) the primary defense for the systems that run the physical process."}]},
{t:"Industrial Protocols",d:"The languages of OT, and their weaknesses.",
obj:["Identify common industrial protocols","Explain the security weaknesses of legacy OT protocols","Explain the implications for OT security"],
read:["OT systems communicate using industrial protocols — Modbus, DNP3, EtherNet/IP, PROFINET, BACnet, and others — designed for industrial control, and most legacy ones share a critical weakness: they were designed for a trusted environment and lack security. Modbus, one of the most common, has no authentication or encryption — any device that can reach a Modbus device on the network can read and write to it (send commands to a PLC), with no verification of the sender. DNP3 (used in utilities) similarly lacked security in its original form (secure variants exist but are not universal). These protocols assume the network is trusted and the communicating parties are legitimate — an assumption that fails when an attacker gains network access.",
"The implications for OT security are significant. Because the protocols lack authentication and encryption, an attacker who reaches the OT network can often directly interact with the control systems — reading process data and, critically, sending commands (the write-to-a-PLC capability) that control the physical process. This is why network access to OT is so dangerous (the reachability concern) and why segmentation (preventing attackers from reaching the OT network — modules two, seven) is the primary defense, since the protocols themselves provide no protection. It also means OT network monitoring can detect malicious protocol activity (unexpected commands, unauthorized write operations — the OT IDS of module nine), and that securing the protocols (secure variants, gateways that add security) helps where possible. Understanding industrial protocols and their weaknesses — the legacy OT protocols that lack authentication and encryption, so network access enables direct control-system interaction — frames why OT network security (segmentation, access control, monitoring) is so critical, since the protocols provide no inherent protection against an attacker who reaches the network."],
ex:"An OT security assessment finds the plant uses Modbus, which has no authentication — so any device reaching the control network can send commands to the PLCs (the write capability that controls the physical process). Because the protocol provides no protection, the security relies entirely on preventing attackers from reaching the network (segmentation) and monitoring for malicious protocol activity (unexpected write commands). The protocol's lack of security made network access the critical risk and segmentation the primary defense — the protocol itself could not distinguish an attacker's commands from legitimate ones.",
refs:[["Industrial protocol security (Modbus, DNP3) references","OT protocol weaknesses."],["NIST SP 800-82","Securing OT communications."]],
kc:[
{q:"Legacy industrial protocols like Modbus share the weakness that they:",o:["Are encrypted","Lack authentication and encryption — any device reaching the network can read and write to control systems","Are too secure","Require passwords"],a:1,x:"Legacy OT protocols assume a trusted environment and lack security, so network access enables direct control-system interaction."},
{q:"Because OT protocols lack security, network access to OT is dangerous because:",o:["It is slow","An attacker who reaches the OT network can directly interact with control systems — reading data and sending commands that control the process","It has no effect","Protocols block attackers"],a:1,x:"With no protocol-level protection, reaching the OT network enables sending commands to control the physical process — hence segmentation is critical."},
{q:"The primary defense given weak OT protocols is:",o:["Trusting the protocols","Segmentation and access control (preventing attackers from reaching the OT network) plus monitoring for malicious protocol activity","Encryption on the process","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Since the protocols provide no protection, keeping attackers off the OT network (segmentation) and monitoring protocol activity are the defenses."}]},
{t:"OT Threats and Notable Incidents",d:"Attacks on the physical world.",
obj:["Describe the OT threat landscape","Explain notable OT incidents and their lessons","Explain the physical consequences of OT attacks"],
read:["OT faces a serious and growing threat, because attacks on OT can cause physical consequences — disrupting critical infrastructure, damaging equipment, and endangering safety — making OT a target for nation-states, and increasingly for criminals (ransomware hitting OT). The threat is distinct because the potential impact is physical: a successful OT attack can halt a power grid, contaminate water, cause an explosion, or damage equipment, with consequences far beyond data loss. Notable incidents demonstrate this reality and are essential lessons.",
"The landmark OT incidents teach hard lessons. Stuxnet (discovered 2010) manipulated PLCs to physically damage uranium-enrichment centrifuges — the first widely-known cyberattack causing physical destruction, demonstrating that OT attacks can cause real physical damage and that even air-gapped OT can be reached (via infected media). The Ukraine power grid attacks (2015, 2016) caused blackouts by compromising the grid's control systems — demonstrating OT attacks disrupting critical infrastructure and affecting populations. TRITON/TRISIS (2017) targeted a safety instrumented system (module six) at a petrochemical plant — a chilling escalation, targeting the safety systems that prevent catastrophic physical harm. These incidents established that OT attacks are real, can cause physical consequences up to endangering lives, and are pursued by capable adversaries — driving the urgency of OT security. Understanding the OT threat landscape and notable incidents — the real, physical-consequence attacks on OT — frames why OT security matters so much (the stakes are physical, up to safety and lives) and provides the concrete lessons (Stuxnet's physical damage, Ukraine's infrastructure disruption, TRITON's safety-system targeting) that ground the discipline in reality."],
ex:"The TRITON/TRISIS malware (2017) targeted a petrochemical plant's safety instrumented system — the system designed to safely shut down the process to prevent catastrophic harm (module six). Had it succeeded in disabling or manipulating the safety system while causing an unsafe process condition, the result could have been an explosion endangering lives. This chilling incident demonstrated that OT attacks can target the very safety systems protecting against physical catastrophe — establishing the extreme stakes of OT security and the need to protect the safety systems specifically.",
refs:[["Stuxnet, Ukraine grid, TRITON/TRISIS analyses","Notable OT incidents."],["Dragos and ICS-CERT threat reports","The OT threat landscape."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT attacks are distinct because they can cause:",o:["Only data loss","Physical consequences — disrupting critical infrastructure, damaging equipment, and endangering safety","No impact","Only downtime"],a:1,x:"OT controls physical processes, so attacks can cause physical harm — halting infrastructure, damaging equipment, or endangering lives."},
{q:"Stuxnet demonstrated that:",o:["OT cannot be attacked","OT attacks can cause real physical destruction (damaging centrifuges) and even reach air-gapped systems","Only IT is at risk","Air gaps are perfect"],a:1,x:"Stuxnet physically damaged centrifuges and reached air-gapped OT — the first widely-known cyberattack causing physical destruction."},
{q:"TRITON/TRISIS was especially chilling because it targeted:",o:["Business data","A safety instrumented system — the system preventing catastrophic physical harm","A web server","Email"],a:1,x:"Targeting the safety system that prevents physical catastrophe was an escalation demonstrating the extreme stakes of OT attacks."}]},
{t:"Safety Instrumented Systems",d:"The last line against physical catastrophe.",
obj:["Explain safety instrumented systems (SIS)","Describe their role in preventing physical harm","Explain protecting SIS from cyber threats"],
read:["Safety Instrumented Systems (SIS) are the systems that safely shut down or bring a process to a safe state when dangerous conditions occur — the last line of defense against physical catastrophe (an explosion, a toxic release, equipment destruction). An SIS monitors for dangerous conditions (excessive pressure, temperature) and, if detected, automatically takes safety action (shutting down the process, opening a relief valve) to prevent harm. SIS are safety-critical: they exist specifically to prevent the worst physical outcomes, and their proper function can be the difference between a safe shutdown and a catastrophe. They are traditionally kept separate from the control systems (a safety layer independent of the control layer) precisely because of their critical role.",
"Protecting SIS from cyber threats is paramount, as TRITON/TRISIS (module five) demonstrated by targeting an SIS. A cyberattack on an SIS could disable it (so it fails to prevent a catastrophe when a dangerous condition occurs) or manipulate it (causing unsafe action or masking a dangerous condition) — potentially enabling physical catastrophe, the most severe possible OT attack outcome. Because of this, SIS deserve the strongest protection: strong isolation (segmenting the SIS from other systems, especially from anything an attacker could reach — the highest-priority segmentation), strict access control, and monitoring, treating the SIS as the crown jewels of OT security (the enclave concept from IC-210 applied to the safety systems). The principle is that the SIS, as the last line against physical catastrophe, must be protected so that an attacker cannot disable or manipulate it — because a compromised SIS during an unsafe condition is the nightmare scenario of OT security. Understanding safety instrumented systems — the safety-critical systems preventing physical catastrophe and the paramount need to protect them from cyber threats — frames the most critical protection priority in OT security, the safety systems whose compromise could enable the worst physical outcomes and which therefore must be defended above all."],
ex:"A petrochemical plant treats its safety instrumented system as the crown jewels of its OT security (the TRITON lesson): the SIS is strongly isolated from all other systems (the highest-priority segmentation), access is strictly controlled and monitored, and it is kept independent of the control layer. This protection ensures that even if an attacker compromises other OT systems, they cannot reach or manipulate the SIS — so the safety system remains able to prevent a physical catastrophe. Protecting the SIS above all defended against the nightmare scenario of a compromised safety system during a dangerous condition.",
refs:[["IEC 61511 (Safety Instrumented Systems) and TRITON analysis","SIS and their protection."],["NIST SP 800-82","Protecting safety-critical OT systems."]],
kc:[
{q:"A Safety Instrumented System (SIS):",o:["Stores data","Safely shuts down or brings a process to a safe state when dangerous conditions occur — the last line against physical catastrophe","Is a web server","Controls business logic"],a:1,x:"The SIS prevents the worst physical outcomes (explosions, toxic release) by taking safety action on dangerous conditions — safety-critical."},
{q:"A cyberattack on an SIS could:",o:["Improve safety","Disable or manipulate it — so it fails to prevent (or causes) a catastrophe, the most severe OT attack outcome","Have no effect","Only lose data"],a:1,x:"Compromising the safety system during a dangerous condition could enable physical catastrophe — the nightmare scenario TRITON targeted."},
{q:"SIS deserve:",o:["No special protection","The strongest protection — treated as the crown jewels, with strong isolation, strict access control, and monitoring","Less protection","Public access"],a:1,x:"As the last line against catastrophe, the SIS must be protected above all (the enclave concept), so attackers cannot reach or manipulate it."}]},
{t:"OT Segmentation and Secure Boundaries",d:"Isolating the process from the threat.",
obj:["Explain OT segmentation strategy","Describe secure OT boundary technologies","Explain zones and conduits"],
read:["Segmentation is the primary OT defense (established throughout), because the OT systems often cannot be hardened, so isolating them from threats is how they are protected. OT segmentation follows the Purdue model (module two) and the IEC 62443 zones-and-conduits concept: dividing the OT environment into security zones (groups of systems with similar security requirements) connected by controlled conduits (the permitted, secured communication paths between zones). The most critical segmentation is the OT/IT boundary (isolating the OT control network from the exposed IT network — module two), followed by segmenting within OT (isolating the safety systems — module six, separating critical control zones, isolating remote sites). The goal is that a compromise anywhere (especially in IT) is contained and cannot reach the critical OT systems.",
"Secure OT boundary technologies enforce the segmentation, and some are specific to OT's needs. Firewalls control traffic between zones. Data diodes (one-way gateways allowing data out but not in — the module-nine monitoring can receive OT data while nothing can send commands in) provide strong one-directional isolation for the most critical boundaries (the OT-out-to-monitoring, or the SIS boundary). Air gaps (physical isolation) are used for the most critical systems, though they are not perfect (Stuxnet crossed one) and are increasingly hard to maintain as OT connects. Secure gateways mediate and control OT/IT communication (the industrial DMZ). Unidirectional gateways and diodes are valued in OT precisely because they can enforce that critical systems cannot receive commands from less-trusted networks — the strongest isolation for the highest-consequence boundaries. Understanding OT segmentation and secure boundaries — the zones-and-conduits strategy and the technologies (firewalls, data diodes, air gaps, secure gateways) that isolate the critical OT systems — frames the primary OT defense, the segmentation that protects the systems that cannot protect themselves by isolating them from the threats, especially at the critical OT/IT and safety-system boundaries."],
ex:"A utility segments its OT per IEC 62443 zones and conduits: the OT control network is isolated from IT through a secure industrial DMZ, the safety systems are in their own strongly-isolated zone, and process data flows out to monitoring through a data diode (allowing telemetry out but preventing any commands in — strong one-way isolation for the critical boundary). When IT is compromised, the segmentation and the data diode prevent the attacker from reaching or sending commands to the OT control and safety systems. The zones-and-conduits segmentation with diodes isolated the critical systems that could not defend themselves.",
refs:[["IEC 62443 zones and conduits, NIST SP 800-82","OT segmentation."],["Data diode and secure gateway technologies","OT boundary enforcement."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT segmentation follows the concept of:",o:["A flat network","Zones (systems with similar security requirements) connected by controlled conduits (secured communication paths) — IEC 62443","No structure","Random division"],a:1,x:"Zones-and-conduits divides OT into security zones connected by controlled conduits, isolating critical systems from threats."},
{q:"A data diode provides:",o:["Two-way communication","Strong one-directional isolation — allowing data out (e.g., telemetry to monitoring) but preventing any commands in","No isolation","Faster networking"],a:1,x:"Data diodes enforce one-way flow, valued in OT for isolating critical systems that must not receive commands from less-trusted networks."},
{q:"The most critical OT segmentation is:",o:["Within Level 0","The OT/IT boundary and the safety-system isolation — protecting the critical control and safety systems from reachable threats","There is none","Between IT servers"],a:1,x:"Isolating OT from the exposed IT network and protecting the safety systems are the highest-priority segmentations in OT."}]},
{t:"OT Monitoring and Detection",d:"Seeing threats without disrupting the process.",
obj:["Explain OT monitoring and its constraints","Describe OT-specific detection","Explain passive monitoring and OT IDS"],
read:["Monitoring OT for threats is essential (the detection discipline, since prevention through hardening is limited), but it must respect OT's constraints — monitoring cannot disrupt the physical process (the safety-and-availability priority), so OT monitoring is typically passive (observing traffic without interacting with the sensitive control systems). OT network monitoring and OT-specific intrusion detection systems (from vendors specializing in OT) passively observe the OT network traffic, understanding the industrial protocols (module four), and detect threats: unauthorized devices, anomalous commands (unexpected write operations to PLCs — the malicious-protocol-activity detection), deviations from the normal process baseline (the baselining discipline from BC-102, applied to OT — OT traffic is often highly regular, making anomalies detectable), and known-bad indicators.",
"OT monitoring has advantages and adaptations. Advantage: OT networks are often more regular and predictable than IT (the same devices communicating in the same patterns to run a stable process), making baseline-deviation detection effective (an anomaly stands out against the regular OT baseline more clearly than in noisy IT). Adaptation: the monitoring must be OT-aware (understanding the industrial protocols and process to detect meaningful anomalies) and passive (not disrupting the process). OT monitoring detects the threats that segmentation might miss (an attacker who reaches the OT network, an insider, a compromised device) by observing malicious activity on the OT network — providing the detection layer that complements the segmentation. It also provides asset visibility (module eleven) as a byproduct. Understanding OT monitoring and detection — the passive, OT-aware monitoring that detects threats on the OT network without disrupting the process — frames the detection layer of OT security, the visibility into OT threats that complements segmentation, adapted to OT's constraints (passive, non-disruptive) and advantages (regular, predictable traffic that makes anomalies detectable)."],
ex:"A utility deploys passive OT monitoring: an OT-aware IDS observes the control network traffic (without interacting with the sensitive systems), understands the industrial protocols, and baselines the regular OT process traffic. When an attacker who reached the OT network sends an anomalous command to a PLC (an unexpected write operation deviating from the regular baseline), the passive monitoring detects it — providing detection that the segmentation alone would not, without disrupting the process. The OT-aware, passive monitoring caught the threat on the OT network, complementing the segmentation.",
refs:[["OT/ICS network monitoring (Dragos, Nozomi, etc.) and NIST SP 800-82","OT detection."],["OT anomaly detection references","Passive, protocol-aware monitoring."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT monitoring is typically passive because:",o:["It is cheaper","Monitoring cannot disrupt the physical process — the safety-and-availability priority requires observing without interacting with sensitive systems","It is faster","Active monitoring is illegal"],a:1,x:"OT's constraints require non-disruptive, passive monitoring that observes traffic without interacting with the sensitive control systems."},
{q:"OT networks are often advantageous for anomaly detection because they are:",o:["Chaotic","More regular and predictable than IT — the same devices in the same patterns, so anomalies stand out clearly against the baseline","Never monitored","Encrypted"],a:1,x:"Regular OT traffic (running a stable process) makes baseline-deviation detection effective; anomalies stand out more than in noisy IT."},
{q:"OT monitoring detects threats that segmentation might miss, such as:",o:["Nothing","An attacker who reached the OT network, an insider, or a compromised device — observing malicious OT-network activity","Only IT threats","Physical intrusion"],a:1,x:"Monitoring provides the detection layer complementing segmentation, catching malicious activity on the OT network (anomalous commands, unauthorized devices)."}]},
{t:"OT Vulnerability and Patch Management",d:"Managing risk where you cannot patch.",
obj:["Explain the OT patching challenge","Describe managing OT vulnerabilities without patching","Explain compensating controls for OT"],
read:["OT presents extreme vulnerability-management challenges, because the standard remedy — patching — is often impossible or dangerous. OT systems frequently cannot be patched: patching requires downtime (halting the physical process — the availability priority), the legacy systems may have no available patches (unsupported), patches may not be validated for the sensitive OT environment (risking process disruption — the safety concern), and the long lifecycles mean systems run for decades with known vulnerabilities. So OT vulnerability management cannot rely on patching the way IT does (IC-202) — a known-vulnerable OT system often must continue operating unpatched, sometimes for years.",
"Managing OT vulnerabilities without patching relies on compensating controls (the compensating-control discipline from BC-101/IC-202, essential for OT). Since the vulnerable systems cannot be patched, the risk is managed by: isolating them (segmentation so attackers cannot reach the vulnerable systems — the primary OT defense, modules two, seven), monitoring (detecting attempts to exploit — module nine), strict access control, and — where patching is eventually possible — carefully-planned, validated patching during rare scheduled maintenance windows (with extensive testing given the process-disruption risk). The OT vulnerability-management discipline accepts that most OT systems cannot be promptly patched and manages the risk through the compensating controls that protect the unpatchable systems, documenting the accepted risk (the risk-acceptance discipline). This is the unpatchable-legacy problem (BC-103/IC-202) in its most acute form — OT systems that cannot be patched, protected instead by isolation, monitoring, and access control. Understanding OT vulnerability and patch management — managing vulnerabilities where patching is often impossible, through compensating controls — frames the OT-specific reality of vulnerability management, the discipline of protecting systems that cannot be patched by isolating and monitoring them, since the standard IT remedy is unavailable."],
ex:"An OT system has a known critical vulnerability but cannot be patched — patching requires halting the process (unacceptable), and no validated patch exists for the legacy system. Rather than accept blind risk or dangerously patch, the utility manages it with compensating controls: the vulnerable system is strongly isolated (segmented so attackers cannot reach it), monitored for exploitation attempts, and access-controlled, with the risk documented and accepted. When patching becomes possible in a future maintenance window, it is carefully validated and applied. The compensating controls protected the unpatchable OT system — the OT vulnerability-management reality.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-82 (OT patch management)","Managing OT vulnerabilities."],["IC-202 compensating controls and BC-101 risk acceptance","Protecting unpatchable systems."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT systems often cannot be patched because:",o:["Patches are cheap","Patching requires downtime (halting the process), legacy systems may have no patches, and patches may risk process disruption","IT patches work fine","OT has no vulnerabilities"],a:1,x:"The availability priority, legacy/unsupported systems, and process-disruption risk make patching OT often impossible or dangerous."},
{q:"OT vulnerabilities that cannot be patched are managed by:",o:["Ignoring them","Compensating controls — isolation (segmentation), monitoring, and access control — plus documented risk acceptance","Dangerous immediate patching","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Since patching is unavailable, compensating controls (isolation, monitoring, access control) protect the unpatchable systems — the OT reality."},
{q:"OT vulnerability management is the unpatchable-legacy problem:",o:["Solved by IT patching","In its most acute form — most OT systems cannot be promptly patched, protected instead by isolation and monitoring","Not relevant to OT","Easily fixed"],a:1,x:"OT epitomizes the unpatchable-legacy problem; it is managed through the compensating controls that protect systems the standard remedy cannot fix."}]},
{t:"OT Asset Inventory and Visibility",d:"Knowing the industrial environment.",
obj:["Explain OT asset inventory challenges","Describe achieving OT visibility","Explain the foundational role of OT visibility"],
read:["OT security, like all security, depends on knowing what you have (the inventory theme from CM-8/IC-202), but OT asset inventory presents specific challenges: OT environments often lack documentation (decades-old systems, undocumented changes), the systems are diverse and specialized (many device types from many vendors), active discovery (scanning) is risky (could disrupt the sensitive systems — the passive constraint), and OT and IT have often been managed separately (so the OT inventory may be poor). Yet you cannot secure, segment, monitor, or manage vulnerabilities in OT systems you do not know exist — making OT visibility foundational, and its frequent absence a serious gap.",
"Achieving OT visibility relies largely on passive discovery (the same passive OT monitoring of module nine that observes the network can identify the devices communicating — building an asset inventory without active scanning that could disrupt the process). Passive OT monitoring thus provides both detection and asset visibility (identifying the OT devices, their communications, and often their vulnerabilities by fingerprinting). This visibility is foundational: it enables the segmentation (knowing what to isolate), the monitoring (baselining the known devices), the vulnerability management (knowing what vulnerable systems exist), and the incident response (knowing the environment). An organization with poor OT visibility cannot effectively secure its OT — it does not know what it is protecting. Achieving comprehensive OT asset visibility, largely through passive means, is therefore a foundational OT-security investment, and its absence (common in OT environments) is often the first gap to close. Understanding OT asset inventory and visibility — the challenge of knowing the OT environment and the passive means of achieving it — frames the foundational visibility that underpins all OT security, the knowledge of the industrial environment without which securing it is impossible, achieved through the passive discovery that OT's constraints require."],
ex:"A utility with poor OT documentation achieves visibility through passive monitoring: the OT-aware monitoring observes the control network and builds an asset inventory (identifying the devices, their types, communications, and vulnerabilities) without active scanning that could disrupt the process. This visibility — previously absent — becomes the foundation for its OT security: it now knows what to segment, what to monitor, what vulnerable systems exist, and its OT environment for incident response. The passive visibility closed the foundational gap; without it, the utility could not have effectively secured OT systems it did not know it had.",
refs:[["OT asset visibility (passive discovery) references","Achieving OT inventory."],["NIST SP 800-82 and CM-8","Foundational OT visibility."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT asset inventory is challenging because:",o:["OT is well-documented","OT often lacks documentation, active scanning is risky (could disrupt sensitive systems), and OT/IT were managed separately","OT has no assets","It is easy"],a:1,x:"Undocumented legacy systems, the risk of active scanning, and separate OT/IT management make OT inventory hard — yet it is foundational."},
{q:"OT visibility is largely achieved through:",o:["Aggressive active scanning","Passive discovery — the same passive monitoring that observes the network identifies the devices without disrupting the process","No discovery","Guessing"],a:1,x:"Passive OT monitoring provides asset visibility without active scanning that could disrupt the sensitive systems — the OT-appropriate means."},
{q:"OT visibility is foundational because:",o:["It is optional","You cannot secure, segment, monitor, or manage vulnerabilities in OT systems you do not know exist","It only helps IT","Visibility is unimportant"],a:1,x:"All OT security (segmentation, monitoring, vulnerability management, IR) depends on knowing the environment — visibility is foundational."}]},
{t:"OT Incident Response",d:"Responding where safety comes first.",
obj:["Explain OT incident response and its constraints","Describe the safety-first response priority","Explain OT-specific IR considerations"],
read:["Incident response in OT (the IR lifecycle from BC-110/AC-305) must adapt to OT's priorities and constraints, with safety and availability coming first. The standard IT response actions (isolating systems, taking them offline, aggressive containment) can be dangerous in OT — taking a control system offline could halt the physical process or, worse, remove control at a dangerous moment (a safety concern), so OT incident response must consider the physical-process and safety implications of every action. The overriding priority is safety: an OT incident response must not create a dangerous physical condition, so containment and response actions are weighed against their physical-process impact, often requiring OT engineering expertise (understanding the process) alongside the security response.",
"OT incident response has specific considerations. The response team must include OT/process expertise (to understand the physical-process implications and ensure safety), the containment must preserve safe operation (isolating a threat without creating a dangerous condition — potentially bringing the process to a safe state deliberately rather than abruptly cutting control), evidence collection adapts to OT (the systems and their constraints), and recovery must safely restore the physical process. The decision-making weighs the security threat against the physical and safety consequences — sometimes the safest response to an OT threat is a deliberate, controlled process shutdown (a safe state) rather than an abrupt containment that could be dangerous. OT incident response thus blends the security IR discipline with OT engineering and a safety-first priority — a distinct capability requiring OT-aware planning and response (the preparation of BC-110/AC-305 adapted to OT). Understanding OT incident response — the safety-and-availability-first response adapted to OT's physical-process constraints — frames how the organization responds to OT incidents, the IR discipline adapted so that the response protects safety and the physical process above all, requiring OT expertise and safety-first decision-making distinct from IT incident response."],
ex:"Responding to a threat in the OT environment, the team does not simply isolate the affected control system as IT IR would — because abruptly cutting control could create a dangerous physical condition. Instead, with OT engineering expertise, they weigh the physical-process implications and bring the process to a safe state deliberately before containing the threat, ensuring the response does not endanger safety. The safety-first, OT-aware incident response protected the physical process — the IT approach of abrupt containment could have created a dangerous condition. The response blended security IR with OT engineering and a safety-first priority.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-82 (OT incident response)","OT IR."],["BC-110/AC-305 IR and crisis management","IR adapted to OT."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT incident response must prioritize:",o:["Confidentiality","Safety and availability — actions must not create a dangerous physical condition or remove control at a dangerous moment","Speed over safety","Data recovery"],a:1,x:"OT IR weighs every action against physical-process and safety implications; safety comes first, unlike IT's typical priorities."},
{q:"The standard IT action of abruptly isolating a system can be dangerous in OT because it:",o:["Is too slow","Could halt the physical process or remove control at a dangerous moment — a safety concern","Improves safety","Has no effect"],a:1,x:"Abruptly cutting control could create a dangerous physical condition; OT containment must preserve safe operation."},
{q:"OT incident response requires:",o:["Only IT skills","OT/process engineering expertise alongside the security response, with safety-first decision-making","No expertise","Ignoring safety"],a:1,x:"OT IR blends security IR with OT engineering and a safety-first priority — a distinct capability from IT incident response."}]},
{t:"OT Remote Access Security",d:"Securing the connections vendors and engineers rely on.",
obj:["Explain why remote access is a primary OT attack vector","Describe secure OT remote-access architecture","Apply least-privilege and monitoring to OT remote access"],
read:["Remote access is one of the most exploited paths into OT environments, because vendors, integrators, and remote engineers routinely need to reach control systems for maintenance, diagnostics, and support — and those pathways, if poorly secured, become the attacker's entry. Multiple major OT incidents (including the 2021 Oldsmar water-treatment intrusion, where an attacker used remote-access software to briefly alter chemical dosing) trace back to weak remote access: shared credentials, unmonitored remote-desktop tools, direct internet exposure of control systems, and vendor connections that bypass segmentation. The historical OT assumption of isolation makes remote access especially dangerous, because it punches a hole straight through the segmentation that is OT's primary defense — a remote-access path that reaches a PLC from the internet defeats the Purdue-model boundaries entirely.",
"Secure OT remote access follows a layered pattern: no direct connections into OT — remote users terminate in the industrial DMZ (module twelve) and are brokered from there through a jump host or secure remote-access gateway; strong multi-factor authentication and per-user (never shared) credentials; least-privilege scoping so a remote session reaches only the specific system and function needed, for a limited time; session recording and real-time monitoring so every remote action is visible and auditable; and just-in-time access that is disabled by default and enabled only for an approved, time-boxed maintenance window. Vendor access is governed by agreement and technically enforced, never left as a standing open door. The principle is that remote access must never erode the segmentation that protects OT — it is granted narrowly, monitored fully, and closed promptly, so the convenience of remote support does not become the attacker's highway into the physical process."],
ex:"A utility replaces vendor VPNs that connected directly to substation PLCs with a brokered model: vendors authenticate with MFA to a gateway in the industrial DMZ, a security operator approves a time-boxed session, the vendor is scoped to only the target device, and the entire session is recorded. When a vendor account was later phished, the attacker reached only the DMZ gateway — with no standing access and no approved session, the path to the PLCs stayed closed, and the recorded-session monitoring flagged the failed authentication.",
refs:[["CISA guidance on securing OT remote access","Brokered, monitored, least-privilege remote access."],["Oldsmar water-treatment incident analysis","How weak remote access enables OT intrusion."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why is remote access an especially dangerous OT attack vector?",o:["It is slow","It can punch a hole straight through the segmentation that is OT's primary defense, giving a remote path to control systems","It improves safety","It has no risk"],a:1,x:"Remote access can bypass the Purdue-model segmentation, giving attackers a direct path to control systems — defeating OT's main defense."},
{q:"Secure OT remote access should terminate:",o:["Directly on the PLC","In the industrial DMZ, brokered through a monitored jump host or gateway — never directly into OT","On the internet","Anywhere convenient"],a:1,x:"Remote users terminate in the DMZ and are brokered through a monitored gateway, so no session connects directly into the OT network."},
{q:"Just-in-time OT remote access means access is:",o:["Always open","Disabled by default and enabled only for an approved, time-boxed maintenance window","Shared among vendors","Never monitored"],a:1,x:"Just-in-time access is off by default and enabled only for approved, time-limited windows — no standing open door for attackers to find."}]},
{t:"OT Backup, Recovery, and Resilience",d:"Restoring the physical process after a disruption.",
obj:["Explain the unique requirements of OT backup and recovery","Describe recovery planning for control systems","Apply resilience principles to OT environments"],
read:["Backup and recovery in OT centers on restoring the ability to safely run the physical process, which makes it different from IT backup in critical ways. The recovery targets include not just data but device configurations and logic: PLC programs (the ladder logic that runs the process), HMI and SCADA configurations, engineering-workstation project files, and controller firmware — because after a ransomware event, a device failure, or a destructive attack, the operator must be able to rebuild controllers and reload the exact logic that runs the plant. NotPetya (2017) and multiple ransomware incidents that spilled from IT into OT showed that organizations without current, tested OT backups faced prolonged operational shutdowns — a manufacturer that cannot reload its PLC logic cannot restart production. Backups must be current, versioned, stored offline or immutably (so ransomware cannot encrypt them), and — critically — validated against the exact device and firmware they will restore to.",
"OT recovery planning extends resilience beyond backups: maintaining spare controllers and known-good firmware/logic images for rapid replacement; documenting the safe startup sequence so the process is brought back online in a controlled, safe order (a botched restart can itself be dangerous); defining manual or degraded operating modes so the process can run safely even while systems are being restored; and testing recovery on representative equipment, since an untested OT backup may not actually restore a controller. Resilience also means designing for graceful degradation — fail-safe states, redundancy for critical controllers, and the ability to isolate and keep running unaffected zones. The goal is that a cyber or equipment failure becomes a recoverable event with a safe, practiced path back to normal operation, rather than an extended shutdown of critical infrastructure — recovery in OT is measured in restored safe production, not just restored files."],
ex:"A manufacturer hit by ransomware that encrypted its IT and reached engineering workstations recovers in hours instead of weeks because it kept offline, versioned backups of every PLC's logic and HMI configuration, maintained spare pre-imaged controllers, and had a documented safe-startup sequence. The team reloads known-good logic to replacement controllers, validates against the correct firmware, and brings the line back up in the practiced order — the tested OT recovery plan turned a destructive attack into a bounded outage.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-82 recovery guidance for ICS","Backup and recovery for control systems."],["NotPetya OT-impact case studies","Why tested OT backups prevent prolonged shutdowns."]],
kc:[
{q:"OT backups must capture, beyond data:",o:["Only spreadsheets","Device configurations and logic — PLC programs, HMI/SCADA configs, and controller firmware — needed to rebuild the process","Nothing else","Only email"],a:1,x:"OT recovery requires restoring the logic and configurations that run the physical process, not just data files."},
{q:"OT backups should be stored:",o:["Online only, writable","Offline or immutable and versioned, so ransomware cannot encrypt them and the correct version can be restored","On the PLC itself","Nowhere"],a:1,x:"Offline/immutable, versioned backups survive ransomware and let the operator restore the exact known-good version to the process."},
{q:"A documented safe startup sequence matters because:",o:["It is faster to skip","A botched or out-of-order restart can itself create a dangerous physical condition — recovery must be controlled and safe","Order never matters in OT","It has no safety role"],a:1,x:"Restarting a physical process out of order can be hazardous; OT recovery brings the process back in a controlled, safe sequence."}]},
{t:"OT Wireless and IIoT Security",d:"Securing wireless links and the Industrial Internet of Things.",
obj:["Explain the risks of wireless in OT environments","Describe securing industrial wireless and IIoT devices","Apply segmentation and monitoring to IIoT"],
read:["Wireless technologies and the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) are expanding rapidly in OT — wireless sensors, remote telemetry, mesh networks for field devices, and internet-connected smart sensors that stream data for analytics — and each addition extends the OT attack surface in ways the historically wired, isolated OT world did not have to consider. Wireless removes the physical-access barrier: an attacker within radio range, or exploiting a weakly secured wireless link, may reach the OT network without ever plugging in. IIoT devices compound the problem because they are often designed for connectivity and features first and security second — shipping with weak or default credentials, unencrypted communications, infrequent patching, and direct cloud connections that can create an unmonitored path between the field device and the internet, bypassing the carefully designed OT/IT segmentation.",
"Securing OT wireless and IIoT applies the same core OT principles adapted to the new medium: strong encryption and authentication on all wireless links (never open or weakly secured industrial wireless); treating IIoT devices as untrusted and segmenting them into their own zone (module twelve's zones-and-conduits) so a compromised sensor cannot reach critical control systems; brokering any IIoT cloud connectivity through the controlled industrial DMZ rather than allowing direct device-to-internet paths; changing default credentials and hardening devices before deployment; and extending passive monitoring (module eight) to the wireless and IIoT segments so anomalous behavior is detected. The governing principle is that the value of wireless and IIoT — visibility, efficiency, remote data — must not come at the cost of the segmentation and isolation that protect the critical process; new connectivity is embraced only inside the same zoned, monitored, least-privilege architecture that protects the rest of OT."],
ex:"A water utility deploys IIoT flow sensors that stream data to a cloud analytics platform. Rather than letting each sensor connect directly to the internet, the utility places the sensors in a dedicated IIoT zone, encrypts their links, changes default credentials, and routes their cloud data out through the industrial DMZ with one-way flow. When a sensor's cloud account was later compromised, the attacker reached only the isolated IIoT zone — the zoning and DMZ kept the path to the control systems closed while the analytics kept running.",
refs:[["Industrial wireless security guidance (ISA/IEC 62443)","Securing wireless links in OT."],["IIoT security best practices","Segmenting and hardening connected field devices."]],
kc:[
{q:"Wireless in OT is risky because it:",o:["Is always secure","Removes the physical-access barrier — an attacker in radio range may reach the OT network without plugging in","Improves isolation","Cannot be attacked"],a:1,x:"Wireless eliminates the physical-connection barrier, letting an attacker within range or exploiting a weak link reach OT remotely."},
{q:"IIoT devices often ship with:",o:["Perfect security","Connectivity-first design — weak/default credentials, unencrypted comms, and direct cloud paths that can bypass OT segmentation","No connectivity","Strong isolation"],a:1,x:"IIoT prioritizes features and connectivity over security, creating weak-credential, unencrypted, direct-to-cloud paths that bypass segmentation."},
{q:"The right approach to IIoT cloud connectivity is to:",o:["Allow direct device-to-internet paths","Broker it through the controlled industrial DMZ and segment IIoT into its own zone, so a compromised device cannot reach control systems","Skip segmentation","Trust every device"],a:1,x:"IIoT is segmented into its own zone and its cloud traffic brokered through the DMZ, so a compromised device stays isolated from critical control."}]},
{t:"IT/OT Convergence and Governance",d:"Managing the merging worlds and the capstone.",
obj:["Explain IT/OT convergence and its security implications","Describe OT security governance and standards","Design an OT security architecture end to end"],
read:["IT and OT are converging — OT systems increasingly connect to IT networks and the internet (for remote monitoring, data analytics, efficiency), and the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) blurs the boundary further. This convergence brings benefits but expands the OT attack surface: the isolation OT historically relied on (air gaps, separation) erodes as OT connects, exposing OT to the IT threat landscape (the IT-to-OT pivot, module two). Managing convergence securely — maintaining strong OT/IT segmentation even as they connect (the industrial DMZ), securing the new connections, and extending security to the converged environment — is a central modern OT-security challenge, because the convergence that brings value also brings the exposure that OT was historically protected from by isolation.",
"OT security governance and standards structure the discipline. IEC 62443 (the leading OT security standard) provides a comprehensive framework (zones and conduits, security levels, requirements for systems and processes), and NIST SP 800-82 provides OT security guidance; these give the standards and structure for OT security governance. The capstone designs an OT security architecture: applying the Purdue model and IEC 62443 zones-and-conduits (segmentation, especially OT/IT and safety-system isolation), protecting the safety systems as crown jewels, deploying passive monitoring for detection and visibility, managing vulnerabilities through compensating controls, adapting incident response for safety, and governing per the standards — all while managing the IT/OT convergence securely. The graded deliverable — creating a network segmentation architecture for a utility environment that insulates PLCs from corporate access points — is exactly this: the OT segmentation that isolates the critical control systems (PLCs) from the reachable IT/corporate network, the primary OT defense. Its quality is judged by whether it isolates the critical control and safety systems from threats (the segmentation), monitors and manages the environment, and prioritizes safety and availability. Understanding IT/OT convergence, governance, and the OT security architecture — managing the merging worlds and structuring OT security per the standards — frames the modern OT-security reality and the integrated architecture that protects the physical-process systems whose compromise has physical consequences, the culmination of the OT-security discipline."],
ex:"A utility designs its OT security architecture: the PLCs and control systems are isolated from the corporate IT network through a secure industrial DMZ (insulating the critical control systems from the reachable corporate access points — the primary defense), the safety systems are protected as crown jewels, passive monitoring provides detection and visibility, vulnerabilities are managed through compensating controls, incident response is safety-first, and the whole is governed per IEC 62443 — while the IT/OT convergence (remote monitoring connections) is secured through the controlled DMZ. The integrated architecture isolated the critical systems from threats while enabling the converged connectivity — the OT-security culmination.",
refs:[["IEC 62443 and NIST SP 800-82","OT security standards and architecture."],["IT/OT convergence security guidance","Managing the merging worlds."]],
kc:[
{q:"IT/OT convergence expands the OT attack surface because:",o:["It has no effect","The isolation OT historically relied on erodes as OT connects to IT and the internet, exposing OT to the IT threat landscape","OT becomes safer","It removes threats"],a:1,x:"As OT connects (for monitoring, analytics), the historical isolation erodes, exposing OT to IT threats — expanding the attack surface."},
{q:"IEC 62443 provides:",o:["A scanner","A comprehensive OT security framework — zones and conduits, security levels, and requirements — structuring OT security governance","A firewall","A patch"],a:1,x:"IEC 62443 is the leading OT security standard, providing the framework (zones/conduits, security levels) for OT security governance."},
{q:"The OT capstone deliverable (insulating PLCs from corporate access points) exemplifies:",o:["OT patching","OT segmentation — isolating the critical control systems from the reachable IT/corporate network, the primary OT defense","Confidentiality first","Ignoring safety"],a:1,x:"Isolating the critical control systems (PLCs) from threats via segmentation is the primary OT defense — the architecture's core."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: An IT team proposes routinely patching and scanning OT control systems as it does IT. The OT concern is:",o:["Nothing","Patching requires downtime (halting the process) and scanning could disrupt sensitive controllers — OT prioritizes availability and safety","It is too slow","It improves safety"],a:1,x:"OT's inverted priorities (availability and safety first) and physical-process constraints make IT-routine actions dangerous in OT."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A corporate IT network is compromised, but the attacker cannot reach the OT control systems. This is because of:",o:["Weak OT","The OT/IT segmentation (the Purdue industrial DMZ) — the most critical OT boundary, isolating control systems from the exposed IT network","No segmentation","Luck"],a:1,x:"The OT/IT boundary segmentation prevents an IT compromise from reaching the safety-critical control systems — the highest-value OT defense."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An OT assessment finds PLCs with no authentication that cannot be upgraded. The security relies on:",o:["The PLCs' own security","Isolating them (segmentation, access control) so attackers cannot reach the components that cannot defend themselves","Encryption on the PLC","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Weak inherent ICS security makes isolation the primary defense for the systems that run the process but cannot protect themselves."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An OT network uses Modbus (no authentication). The critical risk is that:",o:["It is slow","An attacker reaching the network can send commands to PLCs, controlling the physical process — so segmentation is critical","It is too secure","Modbus blocks attackers"],a:1,x:"Legacy protocols lacking security mean network access enables control-system interaction; segmentation (keeping attackers off) is the defense."},
{q:"SCENARIO: The TRITON/TRISIS malware targeted a safety instrumented system. This was chilling because:",o:["It stole data","It targeted the system preventing physical catastrophe — its compromise during a dangerous condition could enable an explosion","It was slow","It only affected IT"],a:1,x:"Targeting the SIS (the last line against catastrophe) demonstrated the extreme stakes — the SIS must be protected above all."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A critical OT boundary must ensure the safety systems cannot receive commands from less-trusted networks. The technology is:",o:["A two-way firewall","A data diode — enforcing one-way flow (data out, no commands in) for the strongest isolation of the critical boundary","No isolation","An air gap that is crossed"],a:1,x:"Data diodes enforce one-way flow, providing the strongest isolation for critical boundaries that must not receive inbound commands."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An OT system has a critical vulnerability but cannot be patched. The management approach is:",o:["Blind risk acceptance","Compensating controls — isolation, monitoring, access control — plus documented risk acceptance, since patching is unavailable","Dangerous immediate patching","Ignore it"],a:1,x:"OT epitomizes the unpatchable-legacy problem; compensating controls protect the system the standard patch remedy cannot fix."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A utility with poor OT documentation needs to know its OT environment. The OT-appropriate approach is:",o:["Aggressive active scanning","Passive discovery — monitoring the network to build an asset inventory without disrupting the sensitive systems","No inventory","Guessing"],a:1,x:"Passive discovery provides OT visibility without the active scanning that could disrupt the process — the foundational OT-appropriate means."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Responding to an OT threat, the team must not abruptly cut control. The safe approach is:",o:["Isolate abruptly as in IT","Bring the process to a safe state deliberately (with OT engineering expertise) before containing — safety first","Ignore the threat","Cut power immediately"],a:1,x:"OT IR prioritizes safety; abrupt containment could create a dangerous condition, so the response is safety-first with OT expertise."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An OT security architecture is judged sound. The core standard is:",o:["It patches everything","It isolates the critical control and safety systems from threats (segmentation), monitors and manages the environment, and prioritizes safety and availability","It prioritizes confidentiality","It scans aggressively"],a:1,x:"Isolating the critical systems from threats (the primary OT defense), monitoring/managing the environment, and safety-first define sound OT architecture."}
]};
